From: virtudude <virtudude@protonmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] steamcompmgr: recover the virtual white point's y on arm64

GAMESCOPE_DISPLAY_VIRTUAL_WHITE is the colour temperature slider's wire format,
and the arm64 Steam client packs it through the same Xlib format=32 LP64 fault
already documented on GAMESCOPE_COLOR_NIGHT_MODE. The client hands
XChangeProperty a float[2] {x, y} -- 8 bytes -- and Xlib reads two 8-byte longs
off it and transmits the low half of each, so element 0 is x and element 1 is an
out-of-bounds read of the client's stack rather than y.

Nothing downstream can tell the difference. calcColorTransform() engages the
chromatic adaptation on `y > 0.01`, so whichever way the overread falls the
slider is wrong: a large positive stack value builds an adaptation to a nonsense
white point, and a small or negative one disables the slider outright. The night
mode atom showed the same element re-rolling per install rather than sitting at
a constant, so neither outcome is stable enough to treat as the normal case.

The pair is recoverable because Steam does not pick it freely -- both coordinates
come off the CIE daylight locus, and the client's 6500K x is D65's -- so x alone
fixes y to within 0.002 across the slider's range through the locus quadratic.
Take element 1 whenever it decodes as a plausible daylight y, which is what a
fixed client would send, and derive it otherwise. That makes the workaround
self-disarming rather than something to remember to remove.

Guarded to arm64: this is a defect in the arm64 Steam client's packing, not in
the property, and the x86 client sends the pair correctly.

The packing analysis and the daylight-locus recovery are virtudude's. The
acceptance test for element 1 is ours and is deliberately wider than the
original, which only treats a y at or below 0.01 as missing: that catches the
half of the failure where the overread lands small or negative, and leaves the
half where a large positive stack value builds an adaptation to a nonsense white
point. Requiring a plausible daylight y covers both.

diff --git a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
index 8672ae7..e237630 100644
--- a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
+++ b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
@@ -6819,6 +6819,33 @@ handle_property_notify(xwayland_ctx_t *ctx, XPropertyEvent *ev)
 		{
 			g_ColorMgmt.pending.outputVirtualWhite.x = santitize_float( bit_cast<float>( user_vec[0] ) );
 			g_ColorMgmt.pending.outputVirtualWhite.y = santitize_float( bit_cast<float>( user_vec[1] ) );
+
+#if defined(__aarch64__)
+			// novadeck: the arm64 Steam client hits the same Xlib format=32 LP64 packing fault
+			// documented at length on GAMESCOPE_COLOR_NIGHT_MODE above. The client hands
+			// XChangeProperty a float[2] {x, y} -- 8 bytes -- and Xlib reads two 8-byte longs
+			// off it, transmitting the low half of each:
+			//     long[0] = {x, y}          -> low half = x
+			//     long[1] = past the buffer -> whatever the client's stack held
+			// So element 1 is NOT the white point's y, it is an out-of-bounds read. Left alone
+			// it is worse than useless: the adaptation below engages on y > 0.01, so a stack
+			// value that happens to be a large positive float builds a chromatic adaptation to
+			// a nonsense white point, and one that happens to be small or negative disables the
+			// colour temperature slider entirely. Either way the slider never does what the UI
+			// says it does.
+			//
+			// The pair is recoverable because Steam does not choose it freely: both coordinates
+			// come off the CIE daylight locus, and its 6500K x is D65's. So x alone determines
+			// y to within 0.002 across the slider's range via the locus quadratic. Take element
+			// 1 only when it decodes as a plausible daylight y -- which is what a client that
+			// has been fixed would send, making this self-disarming -- and otherwise derive it.
+			{
+				const float x = g_ColorMgmt.pending.outputVirtualWhite.x;
+				const float y = g_ColorMgmt.pending.outputVirtualWhite.y;
+				if ( x >= 0.25f && x <= 0.55f && !( y >= 0.20f && y <= 0.45f ) )
+					g_ColorMgmt.pending.outputVirtualWhite.y = -3.0f * x * x + 2.87f * x - 0.275f;
+			}
+#endif
 		}
 		else
 		{
